Yes, you can create a constructor in an abstract class in Java!

🔹 Why?
Even though you can’t create objects of an abstract class, its constructor is still called when a subclass object is created — to initialize the abstract class’s fields.

✅ Example:
java
Copy
Edit
abstract class Animal {
    Animal() {
        System.out.println("Animal constructor called");
    }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
    Dog() {
        System.out.println("Dog constructor called");
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Dog d = new Dog();  // Output:
                            // Animal constructor called
                            // Dog constructor called
    }
}



✅ Summary:
Yes, abstract classes can have constructors.

They are used to initialize common fields or logic.

Constructors of abstract classes are called before subclass constructors.